assessments:
  - discriminant: mcq
    value:
      id: 515-mcq-01
      prompt: A council's new website is built so that it looks identical and works identically whether accessed on a phone, tablet, or desktop browser. Which quality factor does this most directly address?
      options:
        - Availability
        - Compatibility
        - Cultural awareness
        - Pain point reduction
      correct_index: 1
      feedback_correct: |
        Correct. Compatibility is about a digital product working
        consistently across different devices, browsers, and
        operating systems. A site that looks and works identically
        on phone, tablet, and desktop is a compatibility outcome.
        Availability is about the site being up and reachable;
        pain point reduction is about fixing specific user
        frustrations; cultural awareness is about serving diverse
        audiences appropriately.
      feedback_incorrect: |
        The answer is "Compatibility". Compatibility means the
        product works consistently across different devices,
        browsers, and operating systems. Availability is a
        different concept (whether the site is up and reachable).
      difficulty: foundation
  - discriminant: nei
    value:
      id: 515-nei-01
      stimulus: |
        The Bridge Community Food Hub is a volunteer-run charity
        in a densely multicultural part of a large UK city. It
        distributes surplus food to 200 local households a week,
        many of them recent arrivals with limited English, older
        residents with limited digital confidence, and disabled
        residents who rely on screen readers. The hub wants to
        replace its paper sign-up list with a simple web form that
        lets households register for weekly food collections.
        Volunteers will build and maintain the form themselves on
        a modest budget, with no full-time technical staff.
      question: |
        Identify one accessibility consideration and one cultural
        awareness consideration the hub should address when
        designing the registration form, and explain why each one
        matters for this specific user base.
      max_marks: 4
      mark_scheme:
        name_expected: |
          A specific, scenario-anchored accessibility consideration
          AND a specific, scenario-anchored cultural awareness
          consideration, named clearly. A student who names one of
          each hits this mark in full. A student who names two
          accessibility considerations or two cultural awareness
          considerations can still earn partial credit if both are
          well-chosen and clearly named, but full credit requires
          one of each as the question specifies.

          Examples of accessibility considerations that would
          score: proper form-label-to-input pairing so screen
          readers can announce what each field is for; sufficient
          colour contrast between text and background; large
          clickable/tappable targets for users with limited motor
          control or older devices; keyboard navigation support
          for users who cannot use a mouse; clear error messages
          that appear near the field they refer to.

          Examples of cultural awareness considerations that would
          score: a language-selection option supporting the major
          languages spoken in the local area, not just English;
          avoiding idioms, metaphors, or culturally-loaded terms
          in instruction text that may not translate well;
          offering a "name as it appears on your ID" field
          structured to accept non-Western name orders and
          multiple family names; presenting dates in a format
          that is unambiguous to international users.
        explain_expected: |
          For the accessibility consideration: a clear statement
          of the specific barrier it removes and which user group
          in the scenario it helps. For example, "large tappable
          targets" is a statement; "large tappable targets help
          older residents with limited digital confidence and
          possibly less precise touch input to complete the form
          without accidentally tapping the wrong button, which
          would otherwise block them from registering" is an
          explanation that shows which scenario user it serves.

          For the cultural awareness consideration: a clear
          statement of which cultural or linguistic mismatch it
          addresses and which user group in the scenario benefits.
          For example, "a language option" is a statement; "a
          language selector supporting the main community
          languages lets recent arrivals with limited English
          register without needing a volunteer translator, which
          the hub cannot guarantee is available at all times" is
          an explanation that links the feature to the scenario.
        impact_expected: |
          The consequence for the hub's specific mission must be
          clear and linked to the scenario's actual user base
          (recent arrivals with limited English, older residents
          with limited digital confidence, disabled residents
          using screen readers). Examples that would score:
          without proper screen reader support, disabled
          residents cannot register independently and have to
          rely on volunteers, which the hub cannot always
          provide; without multilingual support, recent arrivals
          self-exclude from the service and the hub fails its
          charitable purpose. A student who names features
          correctly but treats the food hub as a generic service
          rather than one serving this specific diverse user base
          does not hit this mark.
      exemplar_answer: |
        One accessibility consideration the hub should address is
        proper label-to-input pairing on every form field. Each
        field (name, address, household size, dietary needs)
        should have a visible label that is programmatically
        linked to its input, so that a screen reader announces
        the purpose of each field to users who cannot see the
        form. This matters specifically because the hub serves
        disabled residents who rely on screen readers. Without
        correct labelling, those residents either cannot register
        at all or have to rely on a sighted volunteer to help,
        which defeats the point of moving away from a paper sign-
        up list.

        One cultural awareness consideration is offering a
        language selector that supports the major languages
        spoken in the local area alongside English, not only
        English. Many of the hub's users are recent arrivals with
        limited English, and a form they cannot read in a
        familiar language is a form they will not complete. The
        hub's charitable purpose is to reach the widest possible
        need in the community, so a form that effectively
        excludes non-English speakers would narrow the service
        down to a subset of the people it exists to serve. The
        selector would need to cover Urdu, Polish, Romanian, and
        Arabic at minimum based on the local demographic, and
        use a visible flag or written language name rather than
        just a drop-down with country codes.
      exemplar_arc:
        - name: |
            Proper label-to-input pairing on every form field.
          explain: |
            Each field (name, address, household size, dietary
            needs) has a visible label that is programmatically
            linked to its input, so that assistive technologies
            such as screen readers announce the purpose of each
            field to users who cannot see the form.
          impact: |
            The hub serves disabled residents who rely on screen
            readers. Without correct labelling, those residents
            either cannot register at all or have to rely on a
            sighted volunteer to help, which defeats the point of
            moving away from a paper sign-up list.
        - name: |
            A language selector supporting the major languages
            spoken in the local area alongside English.
          explain: |
            The selector covers Urdu, Polish, Romanian, and
            Arabic at minimum based on the local demographic,
            using a visible written language name rather than a
            country-code drop-down, so that users whose first
            language is not English can complete the form
            independently.
          impact: |
            Many of the hub's users are recent arrivals with
            limited English. The hub's charitable purpose is to
            reach the widest possible need in the community, so a
            form that effectively excludes non-English speakers
            would narrow the service to a subset of the people it
            exists to serve.
